  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 926 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe weak backdoor cold front has made its way into the eastern areas \ncausing winds to pick up and blow out of an east-southeasterly \npattern. These 10-20 mph winds predominantly impact our western \nmountain slopes through the day tomorrow. A fog advisory is in \neffect until 9 AM MST on the eastern slopes of the Sacs, but the \nwinds should temper widespread fog development any further west. \nBy morning, wind over the Lordsburg Playa will pick up as those \neasterly winds move across the area overnight. With these winds \nadvecting cooler air, morning temperatures will be a few degrees \ncooler across the lowlands in spite of the ridge currently in \nplace which would normally pull warmer air up out of the south. By\ntomorrow afternoon, high temperatures are comparable to seasonal \nnormals for this time of year, and on Tuesday our lowland \nlocations will have a chance to hit 70 again.\n\nAn upper level trough moving into our area through the day Tuesday \nwill tap into the southwesterly moisture. Our area mountains present \nthe best chances for rain with this system late in the day. Winds \nbecome light and variable after this system passes until Friday when \nsouthwesterly winds pick up, gusting to 25-30 mph across the area. \nTemperatures are seasonable if not above normal by the end of the \nwork week. Our next bout of cold air moves into the area overnight \nFriday leaving relevant precip chances north of the area as of\nyet.",
